{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"bosky-victimized-bullfrog","creation_channel":"cli","description":"`pyproject.toml:375` omits the `ARG*` family from `select`, so nothing detects a parameter that is\naccepted and never read. `ruff check --select ARG src/rebar` reports **72** (56 ARG001, 14 ARG002,\n2 ARG005). This backlog concealed three real accepted-then-dropped values, which is the defect class\nthat motivated this run's unwired-functionality sweep.\n\n**Scope: a one-off sweep. No permanent gate is enabled by this ticket** — see \"Revisit\" below.\n\n## Classification (measured, not estimated)\n\n- **43 mechanical** — 37 zero-touch renames to `_param`, 6 with 1-3 internal keyword call-site edits.\n- **17 in two clusters** — one decision each, not seventeen judgments.\n- **12 needing individual judgment**, of which 3 are real defects tracked separately.\n\n### CLUSTER-1 — reconciler leaf handlers (10 violations)\n\n`apply_inbound.py:135,177,205,224` and `apply_outbound.py:92,127,168,190,215`, all dispatched from\n`_LEAVES` in `typed_dispatch.py:56-70` and invoked at `:142` as `handler(mutation, **_leaf_kwargs)`.\n\nTwo hazards that make a naive rename unsafe:\n\n- `client` is passed **unconditionally** (`typed_dispatch.py:137`: `_leaf_kwargs = {\"client\": client}`).\n  Renaming it to `_client` is an immediate runtime `TypeError`, caught by no type checker.\n- `repo_root` is gated by **runtime string introspection** (`typed_dispatch.py:128`:\n  `accepts_repo_root = \"repo_root\" in sig.parameters or _has_var_kw`). Renaming flips this to\n  `False` and the dispatcher silently stops passing it — a cosmetic lint fix changing dispatch\n  behaviour through a string comparison.\n\n**Preferred resolution: normalize the leaves to `**_kwargs`.** This removes all 10 violations\nwithout a rename and decouples dispatch from parameter spelling. Decide once for all ten; do not\nrename piecemeal.\n\n### CLUSTER-2 — `OutboundMapper` Protocol conformance (7 violations)\n\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/backend.py:89,90,91,93` and `adapters/jira_family/outbound_mapper.py:44,45,46`.\nNames are pinned by the Protocol at `_backend.py:370-392`, implemented by two backends, and call\nsites pass **by keyword** (`outbound_field_diff.py:285-287,346-348`). Renaming is a contract break\nacross a Protocol, two backends and two keyword call sites. `suppressed_out`\n(`jira_datacenter/backend.py:93`) is documented as accepted-and-ignored on purpose (ticket 8390) and\nthe Protocol docstring at `_backend.py:379-383` mandates that behaviour.\n\n**Resolution: `# noqa: ARG002` with a Protocol reference.** This is correct code that ARG cannot\nmodel — ruff concedes the general case is blocked on type inference (astral-sh/ruff#15952, open).\n\n## Why no gate\n\n`_`-prefixing is NOT a universally safe remediation here, contrary to a common assumption. Ruff's\nlead maintainer, astral-sh/ruff#10409: *\"You could change `name: str` to `_name: str`, but that also\ncarries some risk, since it will break for users that pass keyword arguments.\"* This codebase proves\nit: Cluster-1 renames break runtime dispatch, Cluster-2 renames break keyword call sites.\n\nA survey of ~15 major Python projects found **no project that adopted ARG and later reverted** — but\nalso that the exclusion is near-universal and always added in the same commit that first enabled\nruff (attrs: `\"ARG\",  # unused arguments are normal when implementing interfaces`; pythondotorg:\n`# Django views/signals/admin methods have required unused args by framework contract`; Home\nAssistant: `# generic callbacks and setup methods create a lot of warnings`). Counter-examples exist\nand matter: beancount/fava runs ARG fully enabled with no exemptions at all, PlasmaPy for 3+ years.\n\nEnabling ARG here would require carrying roughly **17 permanent inline `# noqa: ARG00x`** — a\ndiscretionary per-site exception mechanism, which this project has ruled against in favour of\nrestrictions that are absolute or bounded by an invariant. If Cluster-1 is normalized to `**_kwargs`\nthe irreducible count drops to about 7, which may change that calculus.\n\n## Revisit\n\nRecord the irreducible exception count after the sweep. **The next rebar-janitor run must revisit\nwhether to enable an ARG gate**, using that measured count rather than an estimate. A note is\nrecorded in `.joe-janitor/tools.md`.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The 43 mechanical violations are resolved; renames are verified safe against keyword call\n      sites and against the symbol name appearing **as a string** (`getattr`, `monkeypatch.setattr`,\n      `sig.parameters`), not by semantic search alone\n- [ ] Cluster-1 is resolved by one decision covering all ten leaves; if `**_kwargs` normalization is\n      chosen, `typed_dispatch.py:128`'s introspection still selects the right handlers and a test\n      pins it\n- [ ] Cluster-2 carries justified `# noqa: ARG002` referencing the Protocol, or an equivalent that\n      does not break the Protocol contract or its keyword call sites\n- [ ] The three real defects are NOT folded into this sweep — they are tracked as\n      `7504-a127-e9b0-42bb` (`timeout_s`), `3803-8e04-7c32-4b80` (`close_reason`), and the\n      `acli_cmd` bug\n- [ ] `ARG` is **not** added to `select` in `pyproject.toml` by this ticket\n- [ ] The post-sweep irreducible exception count is recorded on this ticket\n- [ ] No behaviour change: the reconciler dispatch and the outbound mapping produce identical results","id":"fc0c-e218-8b34-4858","parent_id":"b5bc-f4f7-3433-4181","priority":2,"tags":["janitor"],"ticket_type":"story","title":"One-off ARG sweep of src/: 43 mechanical fixes plus two cluster decisions, no gate"},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1786055538143126001,"uuid":"2e7ebb97-e647-44bd-8776-2e0a7dae4774"}